   "extras": {
    "sections": [
     [
      "Abstract",
      "We report peak load measurements from five monitoring stations collected between 2021 and 2023. A pilot survey in 2018 had put the regional peak load at 449.4 kN; we find marked differences between upland and coastal sites."
     ],
     [
      "1. Introduction",
      "Earlier studies of ice-shelf acoustics relied on single-site records [1, 2]. A multi-site campaign makes it possible to separate local effects from regional trends [3]."
     ],
     [
      "2. Methods",
      "Station metadata are listed in Table 1. Measurements follow the protocol of [3]. Values from the 2020 campaign are given in Table 3 for comparison and are not directly comparable with the 2023 campaign because the instruments changed."
     ],
     [
      "3. Results",
      "Table 2 reports the peak load for each station in the 2023 campaign. The largest change relative to 2020 was observed at station M-09."
     ],
     [
      "4. Discussion",
      "The spread between stations exceeds the measurement uncertainty, which suggests that site conditions dominate. Longer records are needed to confirm the trend."
     ],
     [
      "References",
      "[1] Okafor et al. (2017). [2] Lindgren and Yildiz (2019). [3] Szabo et al. (2020)."
     ]
